#!/bin/sh -c exec${IFS}"${0%/*}/py"${IFS}"$0"${IFS}"$@"
"""agent-hub's event bus: an append-only event log + read cursors; the project mailboxes are a projection of it.

Why: communication across projects (and with the owner) can't depend on a session still being alive: sessions left
for a while can't see each other. Nothing here is a long-running subscriber (the schedule runs every 15 minutes and
exits, sessions go idle), so it's pull: each reader records how far it has read (cursor) and reads on from there.
Some events have several readers (e.g. a done for a handoff from the personal assistant must let the personal
assistant close it, a failed job must reach the status page), without each wiring up its own file/CLI.

Storage: .state/bus/bus.db (SQLite WAL, busy_timeout, every write wrapped in a transaction, so several processes
writing at once is safe; not version-controlled, no backup: if it breaks, letters not yet received are lost,
accepted by the owner on 2026-10-21).
  events(seq increasing, id unique = the id the sender gave (resend = nothing happens), ts, type, source,
         to_project, ref, payload JSON)
  cursors(consumer, seq)
Event types (to add one, have a reader first):
  message            a letter. to_project = the recipient address (a project name, agent-hub, or owner = into the
                     personal assistant; the old alias knas is accepted too)
                     payload: type(request|question|info|progress|done|handoff), text,
                              from_session (the sending session, only a hint), to_session (a reply first waits for
                              the asking session),
                              reply_to, handoff_id
  message.delivered  ref = the letter's id; it was delivered into some session's context (payload: session, via)
  message.done       ref = the letter's id; the receiving project has handled it (payload: note)
  job.failed         a scheduled job failed (payload: job, exit, detail; to_project = the job's project for registry
                     jobs)
  merge.done / merge.failed / session.report (merge-branch, lease runner), config.applied (agenthub config apply),
  record (Principal Handoff records): the full list is the type enum of contracts/hubbus.v1.schema.json
Any payload may carry chain and depth (docs/design/event-jobs.md section 7): publish() adds AGENT_HUB_JOB_CHAIN /
AGENT_HUB_JOB_DEPTH, the causal chain of the scheduled run writing it, so event-triggered jobs don't trigger themselves; bridge/dispatch/reconcile-questions
carry others' events and drop it, and dispatch hands each letter's chain to the handler it starts.
meta(generation): a random id fixed when the file is created; a reader's cursor (event jobs) is only valid within it.
A letter's state is a projection, never modified in place: inbox = messages to X that no done event points at yet.

Addresses are projects (names in the project registry projects/*.yaml, agent-hub) and owner, never sessions.

A sandboxed project's mail (enforce.sandbox: policy.mail_outbox; design docs/design/bus-sender.md) never touches the
bus, which is in none of its sandboxes: send/ack/done/publish write one JSON file per letter into its own outbox
(<workspace>/.git/agent-hub/outbox/, which only it can write; the letter id is picked here, OUTBOX_ID), and
inbox/show/thread/open-questions read the view of its own letters (<workspace>/.git/agent-hub/inbox.json). The mailbox
service (dispatch, every minute) moves each outbox file into the bus with source = the outbox's project (a sender named
in the file is never read), checked like the command it stands for, then deletes it; a file it refuses is reported to
the project in a letter and deleted too. It writes each view back after dispatching. The session hooks deliver mail
outside the sandbox, from the bus, to the project the folder belongs to by path.

Usage (in a project directory; the sender defaults to the current directory's project):
  hubbus send --to <project|owner> --type <request|question|info|progress|done> --text "…" [--reply-to <id>]
  hubbus send --to owner --type task --text "…" [--due YYYY-MM-DD] [--context …] [--new-reason …] [--id …]
  hubbus send --to owner --type done|drop --task <key> --text "…"   the owner finished it in this project / not needed
  hubbus send --to owner --type done|drop --question <id> --text "…"   this project's question to the owner was
                                      settled here / isn't needed (the owner answered directly in the session):
                                      the personal assistant takes it off "waiting on you" and won't send an answer
                                      back
  hubbus open-questions [--project P] [--json]   questions to the owner not answered yet and not closed in the
                                      project
  hubbus reconcile-questions          the librarian, nightly: for questions open longer than QUESTION_CHECK_DAYS
                                      days, send a request asking the asking project whether it still needs them
                                      (then again every 7 days); questions from discuss-lease handovers (lease-…) are
                                      managed by the personal assistant itself, not asked
  hubbus inbox [--project P]          letters to this project not done yet (also records delivered)
  hubbus show <id>
  hubbus ack <id>...                  record delivered
  hubbus done <id>... [--note "…"]    handled; for handoffs from the personal assistant, the bridge reports back to
                                      the personal assistant to close them
For hooks / the schedule / other scripts:
  hubbus hook session-start|prompt|post-tool   deliver new letters into the session (stdin is the hook JSON;
                                      failures are always silent)
  hubbus bridge                       the schedule, every 15 minutes: handoffs from the personal assistant →
                                      letters, letters to the owner and dones for handoffs → the personal assistant,
                                      dispatch (same as dispatch), prune letters closed more than 60 days ago
  hubbus dispatch                     the platform handles letters nobody took (launchd, every minute; design
                                      docs/design/mailbox-routing.md); first it takes the sandboxed projects'
                                      outboxes in, last it writes their mailbox views:
                                      conversation (a request/question replying to some session) → if that session
                                      is open, wait CONVERSATION_WAIT minutes for its hook, then resume it in the
                                      background (when the context isn't large) or treat it as a delegation;
                                      delegation → if someone is using the project, wait just as long; then a live
                                      session with activity in this project within FORK_RECENT_H handles it in a
                                      background fork with its context (trial since 10-26); only without one is a
                                      headless agent dispatched (notes capability). agent-hub's letters too. When the
                                      fork is done, the original session gets the fork's last reply and the letters it
                                      sent at its next activity (fork_result).
                                      dispatched letters record message.delivered (via handler|resume|held), and
                                      hooks no longer treat them as work to do
  hubbus thread <id>                  the whole thread this letter is in (oldest first)
  hubbus open-handoffs                handoffs from the personal assistant not closed yet (the librarian applies them
                                      into STATE.md nightly, and marks done only when trustworthy)
  hubbus publish <type> --source S [--to P] [--ref ID] [--payload JSON] [--id ID]
  hubbus status [--json]

The personal assistant's interface (defined on the Muninn side; a change there means changing both sides):
  in:  `muninn handoffs --json` (handoffs waiting to be sent); `--delivered <ids>` only after they're committed
       into the bus. The owner's answers are handoffs with effect=answers, reply_to = the event id of the original
       question, sent back to the asking project.
  out: POST http://127.0.0.1:18795/api/messages (local, not via the tailnet's TLS), Bearer = Muninn's
       data/secrets/ingest_token. {"messages":[{id=event id, project, type=question|progress|done, text,
       handoff_id?}]}; id is the idempotency key, so resending is safe; 400 = one item in the batch is invalid and
       the whole batch is rejected.
  v2 (Principal Handoff 0.3 records, the assistant's contracts/records.v2.md; used only with hub.json
      mailbox.records on, off by default):
      send's task/request to the owner → acts raised, done --task → claimed, drop --task → withdrawn (not needed);
      question → decisions raised (key = the letter id), done --question → claimed with the answer + withdrawn
      (settled), drop --question → withdrawn (not needed); no more v1 letters for these. info/progress stay v1, and so
      does a reply in an asks thread (asks is outside 0.3). The assistant's decisions settled come back as records
      with the asking session and the question on the event. dispatch, every minute: records to the owner are
      POSTed in batches to /api/records (invalid ones record job.failed, no retry); for each project (including names
      before a rename) GET /api/records?project=&after=, and POST /api/records/ack only after they're committed
      into the bus. The first read only sets the starting point (what v1 already delivered isn't taken again).
"""
